        ":activity/id": "00c1cd54-a7af-5240-860b-4432978fec0c",
        ":activity/steps": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186097663,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186097721,
              ":tutorial/content": "Suppose $g$ is differentiable at $a$ and $f$ is differentiable at $b=g(a)$. We prove\n$$ (f\\circ g)'(a)=f'(g(a))g'(a). $$\nDifferentiability of $f$ at $b$ lets us write\n$$ f(b+h)-f(b)=f'(b)h+h\\varepsilon(h), $$\nwhere $\\varepsilon(h)\\to0$ as $h\\to0$; set $\\varepsilon(0)=0$. This identity also holds when $h=0$.\n\nTake $h=g(x)-g(a)$. For $x\\ne a$,\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{x-a}\n=\\left[f'(g(a))+\\varepsilon(g(x)-g(a))\\right]\n\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}.\n$$\nSince differentiability implies continuity, $g(x)-g(a)\\to0$ as $x\\to a$. The bracket tends to $f'(g(a))$, and the remaining quotient tends to $g'(a)$. Their product therefore tends to $f'(g(a))g'(a)$, proving the rule.\n\nThis argument does not divide by $g(x)-g(a)$, which may be zero arbitrarily close to $a$.",
              ":tutorial/id": "d34fc877-5572-53a2-b957-2c5d44a488f3",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 13330,
              ":tutorial/title": "Deriving the Chain Rule"
            },
            ":step/expected-seconds": 75.0,
            ":step/id": "9ad2def5-511c-587f-9847-fa7af8e06405",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 466057
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186097696,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186097785,
              ":tutorial/content": "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\frac{df}{dx} = \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{dg} \\cdot \\frac{dg}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dg}(g^{3}) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(1 + 2x) \\\\\n&= 3g^{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 6g^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ng(x) = 1+2x\n$$\n\nfor $g$, we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= 6(1 + 2x)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson.",
              ":tutorial/id": "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 415,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
